{
  "what": "the block terraces up the seaward bank of 389 W Bluff Rd, lowest wall first. Where the ground steps up, how far, and how many courses of block that is.",
  "baked_by": "PointRobertsEngineering site/bake-oceanview-lidar.py",
  "baked": "2026-08-23",
  "measured": "every line and every height, from the WA DNR cascades_north_wali_2023 clip. A riser is a run of cells climbing faster than 0.42 in 1, and it is the gap between the flat under it and the flat over it. The block is 200 mm on the course and 400 on the face, counted off the photographs against a loose block lying on the concrete.",
  "not_measured": "the wall face itself. A flight looks down and a riser is vertical, so no flight will ever hold one. The setback of 0.06 m a course is the ordinary batter for this block. Where each wall stops and starts is not known either: they are drawn unbroken along whatever length of frontage the step runs, and the photographs show one of them down and a stair cut through them. Anything a wall climbs past 12 courses is carried as bank_above_m and no wall is claimed for it.",
